WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.653
Week five.

00:00:01.703 --> 00:00:03.551
Managing the evolution of brands,

00:00:03.601 --> 00:00:04.719
with Shannon Wright,

00:00:04.769 --> 00:00:07.773
Head of Marketing for Confectionery at Nestlé Oceania.

00:00:07.823 --> 00:00:10.306
She leads KitKat and Allen's.

00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:13.132
First, career advice.

00:00:13.182 --> 00:00:14.877
Be interested in the unfamiliar,

00:00:14.927 --> 00:00:16.139
know what matters to you,

00:00:16.189 --> 00:00:17.153
stay curious,

00:00:17.203 --> 00:00:18.521
and be a good person.

00:00:18.571 --> 00:00:20.782
The industry is small.

00:00:21.467 --> 00:00:23.776
Two brands, opposite problems.

00:00:23.826 --> 00:00:26.051
KitKat takes the consistency path.

00:00:26.101 --> 00:00:30.265
Allen's must define its long-term path as trends shift underneath it.

00:00:30.967 --> 00:00:32.780
KitKat stands for a break.

00:00:32.830 --> 00:00:34.655
It was made for workers on their break,

00:00:34.705 --> 00:00:36.483
and the bar takes two hands to snap,

00:00:36.533 --> 00:00:37.989
so you stop.

00:00:38.700 --> 00:00:40.266
Three truths line up.

00:00:40.316 --> 00:00:42.412
Brand belief: breaks are good.

00:00:42.462 --> 00:00:44.924
Audience truth: everyone needs one.

00:00:44.974 --> 00:00:47.954
Product truth: it's made to be broken.

00:00:48.667 --> 00:00:51.766
The most dangerous person in marketing is the bored brand manager,

00:00:51.816 --> 00:00:54.042
who changes things that didn't need changing.

00:00:54.092 --> 00:00:55.905
Have a strategy.

00:00:56.600 --> 00:00:58.637
Consistency is a superpower.

00:00:58.687 --> 00:01:01.420
The need hasn't changed, but the tension has.

00:01:01.470 --> 00:01:03.909
Sixty three percent of Gen Z need a break,

00:01:03.959 --> 00:01:06.646
yet sixty four percent can't switch off.

00:01:07.333 --> 00:01:10.797
They sit down, doom scroll, and their minds stay busy.

00:01:10.847 --> 00:01:15.555
So KitKat's job now is the emotional break, not just the physical one.

00:01:16.267 --> 00:01:21.099
Distinctive brand assets shortcut people to you: the bar, the red, the snap.

00:01:21.149 --> 00:01:22.785
Use them or lose them.

00:01:22.835 --> 00:01:27.537
Even Have a, unfinished, works, because your brain closes the gap.

00:01:28.233 --> 00:01:31.909
The second most dangerous person is the brand manager with no ambition.

00:01:31.959 --> 00:01:34.044
Cultural relevance is a currency.

00:01:34.094 --> 00:01:38.615
Impact comes from brand size, creative quality and budget setting.

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:41.267
Fans argued over the right way to eat a KitKat:

00:01:41.317 --> 00:01:42.117
around twenty seven million social engagements.

00:01:42.142 --> 00:01:48.626
A debate about your brand is a jackpot, so fan the flames.

00:01:49.333 --> 00:01:50.722
Four principles.

00:01:50.772 --> 00:01:52.715
Meet your audience where they are.

00:01:52.765 --> 00:01:54.790
Tone and timing are everything.

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:56.630
Be clear on the brand's role.

00:01:56.680 --> 00:01:59.763
And subvert your equity to be one of the people.

00:02:00.467 --> 00:02:01.726
Now Allen's.

00:02:01.776 --> 00:02:02.752
People love it,

00:02:02.802 --> 00:02:05.983
but a brand is a leaky bucket that drains unless you refill it.

00:02:06.033 --> 00:02:09.073
We want a playground, not a museum.

00:02:09.767 --> 00:02:13.066
Gen Z want intense flavours and surprising textures.

00:02:13.116 --> 00:02:15.979
Forty five percent of Australians say texture appeals,

00:02:16.029 --> 00:02:19.381
and lolly salads turn sweets into social currency.

00:02:20.067 --> 00:02:24.062
GLP-1 drugs could reach fifteen percent of Australians by year end,

00:02:24.112 --> 00:02:25.583
dulling the joy of sweets.

00:02:25.633 --> 00:02:30.401
And India has overtaken England for migration, bringing new tastes.

00:02:31.100 --> 00:02:31.900
When Arnott's changed four Shapes flavours, people were outraged.

00:02:31.454 --> 00:02:37.489
A heritage brand doesn't have to taste better.

00:02:37.539 --> 00:02:39.658
It has to taste the same.

00:02:40.367 --> 00:02:43.902
Know your consumers, potential consumers and brand.

00:02:43.952 --> 00:02:45.777
Then test any stretch for fit,

00:02:45.827 --> 00:02:46.885
distance from the core,

00:02:46.935 --> 00:02:48.076
consumer permission,

00:02:48.126 --> 00:02:49.468
risk versus reward,

00:02:49.518 --> 00:02:51.229
and keep monitoring.

00:02:51.933 --> 00:02:52.933
For Heinz,

00:02:52.983 --> 00:02:54.312
soup is a near step,

00:02:54.362 --> 00:02:55.987
pasta sauce a credible stretch,

00:02:56.037 --> 00:02:57.378
vodka the exception.

00:02:57.428 --> 00:03:01.283
Or collaborate, like Allen's with Pepsi and Mountain Dew.

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:03.990
So: protect your assets, and know why you're entering culture.

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:07.976
Know your consumers and who you are.

00:03:08.026 --> 00:03:11.266
Use AI to ease the work, not do the thinking.

00:03:11.316 --> 00:03:13.150
That's week five.
